sudo apt-get install exfat-utils

# All commands not proceeded by a # are commands that need executed in a shell

# To stop RPI prompt from appearing to confirm you want to run the program that the desktop icon launches:
/usr/bin/pcmanfm

# Once PC MAN FM loads perform the following:
# Edit] --> [Preferences] --> [General]
# [x] Don't ask options on launch executable file

# Install one test station server in a network
# Multiple client test stations can be installed in a network and they need configured
# to point to the test station server

# install client test station logic as root:
sudo sh -x install_test_station.sh -c

# install server test station logic as root:
sudo sh -x install_test_station.sh -s

# to control the dhcp server which does not currently startup at boot time
sudo service isc-dhcp-server start
sudo service isc-dhcp-server stop
sudo service isc-dhcp-server restart

# to remove black borders on screen
sudo nano /boot/config.txt
uncomment line below:
# disable_overscan=1
then reboot

Install and Configure Windows 10 Node Controller Test Station

To copy the Windows 10 System to the new 500 MB Kingston SSD do the following in command prompt:
diskpart
list disk
select disk 0
detail disk
clean
convert gpt
exit

Download and install easseus todo home application to copy Windows 10 system files to 500 MB SSD
License key: GVZCX-715V2-REYRD-FGQHU-2MTFP
Launch program
Select System Clone
Select the 447 GB Kingston SSD as the destination of the clone
Select Advanced Options button and check Optimize for SSD and Sector by Sector Clone and select Ok
Select Next at bottom of screen and copy will start

Resize Windows drive to use all 447 GB of Kingston SSD
Launch diskmgmt.msc from terminal or Run command
Extend the the Windows 10 Volume to use all of the 447 GB of the Kingston drive by doing the following:
Right-Click on windows:A on Disk 0:
Select Extend Volume from drop down menu
Select Next
Select Next on the next screen

windows:A should now be 447 GB

Reboot Windows
Hold F7 down to go into the BIOS
Change boot order so Boot Manager(P0: Kingston if first device to boot from)

Install Rasclock 4 into all RPIs so they have a real-time clock
Follow instructions here:
https://afterthoughtsoftware.com/products/rasclock

sudo rasp-config
Interfacing Options->Enable i2c interface

Specifically do the following for Raspbian Buster image:
Edit /boot/config.txt
Add the following line:
dtoverlay=i2c-rtc,pcf2127

Edit /lib/udev/hwclock-set
Comment out the lines:
if [ -e /run/systemd/system ] ; then
    exit 0
fi

Comment out all lines with -ssystz in them

Set the os system time with following command:
sudo date -s "YYYYMMDD hhmm"
sudo hwclock -w
